                <pb facs="0130" n="100" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v08/pg100"/>

                <head>36. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On February 4, 1965, in response to a question at a press conference, French
                    President Charles de Gaulle called for a gradual return of the world monetary
                    system to the gold standard. De Gaulle called for nations having international
                    financial responsibilities to consider paying off their balance-of-payments
                    debts solely in gold. Gold, he said, was the only “unquestionable monetary basis
                    which did not bear the mark of any individual country,” and therefore the most
                    equitable standard upon which to conduct international financial transactions.
                    For text of his statement, see <hi rendition="#italic">American Foreign Policy:
                        Current Documents, 1965,</hi> pages 219–221.</p>
                <p>A February 4 Treasury Department statement objected to President de Gaulle’s
                    proposal to revert to the full gold standard, arguing that such a monetary
                    system had “collapsed” in 1931 and was “incapable of financing the huge increase
                    of world trade” that marked the 20th century. For text, see ibid., page 221.
                    Secretary Dillon sent a copy of the press release under cover of a February 4
                    memorandum to President Johnson. (Johnson Library, Bator Papers, Balance of
                    Payments Message, February 10, 1965, Memos [1 of 2], Box 16)</p>
            </div>
